This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
## 1. Primary Request and Intent

The session began with a single bug report and evolved through many rounds of debugging, hardening, and reflection, ending in an explicit two-phase request that is the immediate active work:

- **Immediate, most recent request** (verbatim): *"This sounds a lot more sensible. Let's scope it our and prepare a fix please. Once done do a very deep analysis and thorough check of the code for similar mistakes we've made. Places where the exact same thing is implemented twice and places where we're complicating things unecessary. Use agent teams that are very targeted at identifying these types of issues. Then bring those back and analyze patterns and consider our overall architecture along with semantic models to propose comprehensive fixes that consider the whole vs just the issue one at a time. Present the findings and proposals."*
  - This has two phases: (A) implement the "persist-and-verify" redesign of release-commit decision verification (currently in progress), (B) after A is done, run a deep multi-agent audit of the whole codebase for (i) duplicate/parallel implementations of the same logic (drift risk) and (ii) unnecessary complexity, then synthesize architectural findings and present a comprehensive proposal.
- Earlier requests, all substantially completed: fix the release-PR camelCase serialization bug (PR #47, merged); harden the release-PR action's test suite to use the real binary, not stubs; migrate the release-PR executor from `git push` to GitHub's forge commit API (PR #49, merged) to avoid the `GITHUB_TOKEN` `.github/workflows/*` write restriction on public repos; fix a stuck release by reverting an unsigned merge (PR #50) and disabling rebase-merge repo-wide (squash-only enforced via `gh api -X PATCH`); clean up "AI slop" wording in workflow/spec files (PR #52, Tier 1 applied; Tier 2 spec-ID migration paused mid-discussion); fix a fixed-group cascade-authorization bug in `derive_release_commit_decision` (PR #53, opened but about to be superseded); set up a permission allowlist in `.claude/settings.json`; clean up the user's real local repo (`~/Projects/callisto`) to be on `main` with only legitimate changes.
- User explicitly told me to work in `~/Projects/callisto` (their real repo) going forward rather than the scratch worktree, after I explained I'd been using `/tmp/callisto-relpr-debug` to avoid disturbing their in-progress branch.
- User explicitly corrected my habits: stop prefixing every Bash command with `cd <dir> &&`, and use `rg` instead of `grep`.

## 2. Key Technical Concepts

- **Rust workspace** managed by Cargo + moon + just, crates: `callisto-model` (Layer 1, permissive license, no AGPL deps), `callisto-vcs`, `callisto-graph`, `callisto-cli`, `callisto-changelog`, `callisto-format`, `callisto-manifests`, `callisto-conventional`, `callisto-moon`, `callisto-fixtures`.
- **serde `rename_all` gotcha**: `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase", tag = "kind")]` on an internally-tagged enum only renames the `kind` discriminant value, NOT fields inside variants — must add `rename_all_fields = "camelCase"` (serde ≥1.0.166) to also rename variant-internal fields. Found and fixed in TWO places this session: `ReleasePrActionV1` (`callisto-model/src/release_pr.rs`) and `ReleaseInclusionReason` (`callisto-model/src/release.rs`).
- **Fixed/linked groups**: `callisto.toml`'s `[[fixed-group]] name = "workspace" members = [...]` forces lockstep versioning — `solve_cascade` (in `cascade.rs`) converges ALL members to one shared target version the instant any member bumps (a design documented as "Track 1: Fixed group convergence").
- **Release-commit trust boundary**: `release-candidate` GitHub Actions job requires a commit to be BOTH (a) associated with a merged PR whose branch matches `callisto/version-packages` or `callisto/version-packages--<40hex>`, AND (b) GitHub-verified/signed (`commit.verification.verified == true`). Only squash-merge and merge-commit strategies produce GitHub's own signature (`committer: "GitHub"`); rebase-merge does not (`committer: <the human>`, `verified: false`).
- **GITHUB_TOKEN restriction on public repos**: cannot write `.github/workflows/*` via git push OR via GraphQL `createCommitOnBranch`'s `fileChanges`, on any ref. A ref MOVE (not a content write) is not subject to this restriction — this is the basis of the forge-commit-API executor redesign (PR #49).
- **"Persist and verify" vs "recompute and hope it matches"**: the core architectural insight of the current work. `ReleaseDecisionV1` (in `callisto-model/src/release.rs`) already has a `digest: DecisionDigest` field computed via `decision_digest()`/`CanonicalTranscript::decision_v1()`, and its custom `Deserialize` impl already re-validates entries and rejects any decision whose digest doesn't match — a ready-made tamper-detection primitive that was never wired into the automated release-PR flow.
- **changesets/knope comparison**: verified via actual source (`changesets/action`'s `src/run.ts`/`src/publish/index.ts`) that `changeset publish` only diffs current on-disk versions against the npm registry — no git-diff/changeset-provenance re-verification at all. This is simpler but less rigorous than what Callisto attempts.
- **ApplyPermit** pattern: `ApplyPermit::granted_unless_dry_run(global.dry_run)` gates all real file writes; `atomic_write(path, content, permit)` in `callisto_model::atomic`.
- **clap CLI arg constraints**: `conflicts_with`, `required_unless_present`, `requires` attributes used to encode mutual-exclusivity/dependency between `--package`/`--from-release-commit`/`--decision` flags on `release plan`.
- **Diagnostic/error codes**: `GraphError` (in `callisto-graph/src/error.rs`) and `ReleasePrDecisionError`/other model errors use `#[diagnostic(code(EXXX))]` with a compile-time uniqueness check (`DIAGNOSTIC_CODE_SOURCE_FILES` in `callisto-model/src/lib.rs`) across specific files.
- **Squash-merge commit message convention**: GitHub's default squash-merge commit message ends in `(#N)` — used as forensic evidence to distinguish squash from rebase-merge when API doesn't expose merge strategy directly.
- **Git worktrees**: used `/tmp/callisto-relpr-debug` (main work), `/tmp/callisto-relpr-specs` (parallel specs/docs work, later merged and removed), `/tmp/callisto-verify-fix` (one-off verification, removed) before switching to the user's real `~/Projects/callisto` directory per their explicit request.
- **Agent/fork orchestration**: used `Agent` tool with `subagent_type: "fork"` for a code-implementation task (context-inheriting background worker) and `subagent_type: "general-purpose"` for an independent specs/docs worker, run in parallel via a single message with two tool calls. Also used `courier:pr-narrator` agent via the `/courier:pr` skill for PR title/body drafting.
- **Artifact tool**: published an HTML proposal document ("Wording Cleanup Proposal") to `https://claude.ai/code/artifact/8bbc782b-6a05-47a8-b8c0-f5f730965d9c` using diff-style (git-diff red/green inspired) visual design per the `artifact-design` skill.

